@import "./compiler.sly"
@use LSP

class SlytherLanguageService {
    What an editor asks about Slyther scripts, answered from paths and positions alone, with no protocol
    in it, so it is tested as any class. It is created with what the editor holds for a path, or nothing
    when the file is not open, so the disk is read instead, and it forgets everything analyzed when told
    something changed.

    The project of a file is the nearest main.sly up the tree whose imports reach the file, or the file on
    its own when none does. It is parsed leniently with #{Compiler::SlytherParser}, reading what the editor
    holds, and when nothing is wrong with what it parsed, its kinds and scripts are grouped with
    #{Compiler::SlytherArtifactKind} and #{Compiler::SlytherRunScript} as well, so what is wrong with their
    shape, and every warning, is reported on the declaration the message names, or on the first line of
    the entry point when it names none. What it answers comes from the #{Compiler::SlytherSourceMap} of
    the project:

    - diagnostics: What is wrong in a file, in the order found
    - symbols: The declarations of a file as a tree, a `Parent::child` declared in the same file under its
      parent, named by what follows the parent's name, each with its kind, its lines and the columns of
      its name
    - workspace symbols: Every declaration of the project whose name holds a query, ignoring case
    - folding: The lines every block of a file may fold, from the line it opens on to the one before it
      closes, for the blocks that span more than two lines
    - definition: Where what is written at a position is declared: the name of a declaration is its own,
      a reference, the type of an arg or the kind that opens a declaration goes to what it resolves to,
      every time it is declared, and a path goes to the file it names, when it is one
    - references: Everywhere the declaration written at a position, or the one the reference at it
      resolves to, is referenced, in prose, as a type or as a kind, and where it is declared when asked
    - hover: The head of the declaration written or referenced at a position as it is written, then its
      prose cut short after twelve lines, as markdown, or the path a position is on and whether it exists
    - completions: What may be written at a position, given the line up to it: after `#{`, every
      declared name and, for a name inside the scope the line resolves in or one enclosing it, the
      shorter name that resolves to it; after `@use`, the namespaces; after the colon of an arg, the
      built-in types and the names again; after the colon of a declaration, the qualifiers; and on a
      line that holds nothing else yet, the kinds the block around it may contain, or, inside no block,
      the declared kinds and the directives. Every item says the columns it replaces, what was written
      of it so far
}

class SlytherLanguageServer {
    Serves the language server protocol over a pair of streams, stdin and stdout unless given others,
    handing every request to a #{SlytherLanguageService} that reads the documents the editor holds open,
    and translating paths and positions to what the protocol wants: uris, ranges, symbol kinds and
    completion kinds, each completion as an edit of the columns it replaces. It offers incremental
    synchronization, completion triggered by a brace, an at sign, a colon and a space, hover, definition,
    references, document and workspace symbols and folding ranges.

    Once a document opens, changes or closes it forgets what the service analyzed, waits a moment for
    typing to settle, and reports what is wrong in every open document, clearing what it reported for a
    document that closed. Since stdout carries the protocol alone, once it listens anything printed goes
    to stderr.
}
